What it shows. With the two optimizing compilers off, a warmed process reads 1.04 times what a fresh one does, the
same for every construct, where with them on it read 2.23 to 5.71 times (stack-share-probe.out): the compilers are
what makes a warm process read deeper. With three quarters of the stack a warmed process then reads 0.78 of the fresh
limit in every row, so a bound by the stack really used would be sound there, by a margin that does not depend on the
construct. Its price: a generation takes 31.7 s against 11.6 s. Either compiler alone off costs nothing, and was not
measured for what it leaves of the difference.
